Gears of War 3: host occlusion queries that work. The Xenos GPU writes a query's begin and end
counters to two separate blocks, and D3D subtracts them; the end block carries the game's
"finished" marker. The runtime paired an end only with a begin at the same address, so the first
query of a session turned host queries off, and from then on every query answered "visible": the
game culled nothing behind walls (about 2900 draws a frame in a room that needs a few hundred) and
drew lights through them. An end now pairs with the open query, a begin over an open one drops it,
and the begin block is cleared. Results are written once the GPU has finished the submission
(polled at each query and swap, and all of them before the command processor waits), instead of a
CPU wait on the GPU for every query. Every 5 seconds an OCCLUSION line counts host results,
occluded ones, fake "visible" answers and packets handled with host queries off; each path that
turns host queries off logs why.

